tion So yes, a tiny taste of something “deadly” usually won’t kill you, but that doesn’t mean the plant is safe or that the word “deadly” is wrong. The word exists because if you eat a meal’s worth of that plant, you die. The universal edibility test works precisely because it avoids ever reaching the meal-sized dose; it stays in the micro-dose range where the label “poisonous” hasn’t yet been empirically confirmed by a corpse. But once our ancestors confirmed that a normal serving killed someone, the plant earned the label “poisonous” forever—a cultural mutation that improved survival. The label is the lesson, not a description of the toxin’s absolute properties at every conceivable fraction of a gram. In short, we don’t call deadly things “safe in small amounts” because that phrasing invites the very confusion that gets people killed. “Poisonous” is a far better survival heuristic: it says “never consume enough to matter,” and that’s exactly the lesson our ancestors learned the hard way.
